# File Organization & Structure Rules

## Project Structure Standards

### Directory Organization
- **Root Level**: Keep root directory clean with only essential files
- **Source Code**: Organize source files in logical directories (main/, components/, functions/)
- **Assets**: Separate assets by type (images/, styles/, scripts/)
- **Documentation**: Keep documentation in dedicated folders (docs/, README files)

### File Naming Conventions
- **Descriptive Names**: Use clear, descriptive file names that indicate purpose
- **Consistent Case**: Use kebab-case for file names (e.g., `user-profile.js`, `data-processor.js`)
- **Extensions**: Use appropriate file extensions (.js, .html, .css, .json)
- **Special Files**: Use conventional names for special files (.env, .gitignore, package.json)

### Component Organization
- **Component Folders**: Group related components in dedicated folders
- **Component Files**: Use PascalCase for component file names (e.g., `UserProfile.js`)
- **Index Files**: Use index.js files to export components from folders
- **Asset Co-location**: Keep component-specific assets with their components

## Code Organization Standards

### Import/Export Structure
- **Import Order**: Group imports by type (external libraries, internal modules, relative paths)
- **Named Exports**: Use named exports for better tree-shaking
- **Default Exports**: Use default exports for main component files
- **Barrel Exports**: Use index files to re-export multiple modules

### File Content Organization
- **Header Comments**: Include file description and author information
- **Import Section**: Group all imports at the top of the file
- **Constants**: Define constants after imports
- **Helper Functions**: Place helper functions before main functions
- **Main Logic**: Keep main business logic organized and readable

### Module Structure
- **Single Responsibility**: Each module should have one clear purpose
- **Cohesion**: Keep related functionality together in the same module
- **Coupling**: Minimize dependencies between modules
- **Interface Design**: Design clean interfaces between modules

## Asset Management

### Image Organization
- **Format Selection**: Use appropriate image formats (PNG, JPG, SVG, WebP)
- **Naming Convention**: Use descriptive names with size indicators when needed
- **Optimization**: Optimize images for web use
- **Responsive Images**: Organize responsive image variants logically

### Style Organization
- **CSS Architecture**: Use consistent CSS architecture (BEM, SMACSS, or utility-first)
- **Component Styles**: Keep component styles with their components when possible
- **Global Styles**: Maintain global styles in dedicated files
- **Theme Management**: Organize theme variables and configurations

### Script Organization
- **Utility Functions**: Group utility functions in dedicated files
- **Service Classes**: Organize service classes by domain
- **Configuration**: Keep configuration in separate files
- **Entry Points**: Maintain clear entry points for different parts of the application

## Documentation Standards

### README Files
- **Project README**: Comprehensive project overview and setup instructions
- **Component READMEs**: Document complex components with usage examples
- **API Documentation**: Document all public APIs and interfaces
- **Setup Instructions**: Clear setup and installation instructions

### Code Documentation
- **Inline Comments**: Comment complex logic and business rules
- **Function Documentation**: Document function parameters, return values, and behavior
- **Class Documentation**: Document class purpose, methods, and properties
- **API Documentation**: Document all public methods and expected inputs/outputs

### Version Control
- **Commit Messages**: Use descriptive commit messages following conventional commits
- **Branch Naming**: Use consistent branch naming conventions
- **File Tracking**: Properly track all source files and exclude build artifacts
- **History Management**: Maintain clean git history with meaningful commits

## Build & Deployment Organization

### Build Configuration
- **Build Scripts**: Organize build scripts in package.json
- **Environment Configs**: Separate configuration for different environments
- **Build Outputs**: Organize build outputs in dedicated directories
- **Asset Pipeline**: Maintain consistent asset processing pipeline

### Deployment Structure
- **Environment Files**: Keep environment-specific files organized
- **Deployment Scripts**: Organize deployment scripts and configurations
- **Backup Strategy**: Maintain organized backup and recovery procedures
- **Monitoring**: Organize monitoring and logging configurations

## Quality Assurance

### Code Review Standards
- **Review Checklist**: Use consistent code review checklists
- **File Organization**: Review file organization as part of code reviews
- **Naming Conventions**: Ensure consistent naming across the project
- **Documentation**: Verify documentation is updated with code changes

### Testing Organization
- **Test Structure**: Organize tests to mirror source code structure
- **Test Naming**: Use descriptive test names that indicate what is being tested
- **Test Data**: Organize test data and fixtures logically
- **Coverage**: Maintain organized test coverage reports
